# nbks/ — analysis notebooks

Exploratory analyses kept as plain `.py` scripts in the percent/py:percent
style (git-diffable{% if has_corpus_pipeline %}, runnable headless via `make nbks-corpus`{% endif %}, openable as
notebooks in VS Code/Jupytext).
{% if has_corpus_pipeline %}
- `corpus_analysis.py` — corpus statistics for the ingested article set
  (sizes, categories, token distributions) to sanity-check ingestion before
  trusting retrieval quality numbers.
{% endif %}
{%- if is_ml_shaped %}
- `01_eda.py` — the first hour of the modelling problem. Bivariate-first:
  every section compares a feature *against the target* rather than
  cataloguing columns in isolation. Point the four parameters at the top
  (`DATA`, `SEP`, `TARGET`, `DROP`) at your frame and work down.

  Two cells are load-bearing and easy to skip. The **leakage audit** runs
  before any correlation work, because a leaked feature does not raise an
  error — it returns a great score and reads as success. The **handoff cell**
  at the bottom is this notebook's actual output: the `CATEGORICAL` /
  `NUMERIC` / `ORDINAL` / `EXCLUDED` lists that feed
  `{{ ml_source_root }}/selection/`. It reconciles its own counts, so a column
  nobody decided about is reported rather than silently dropped.
{%- endif %}
{%- if not has_corpus_pipeline and not is_ml_shaped %}
No notebooks ship for this project shape yet — drop new analyses here as `.py`
scripts.
{%- endif %}

Convention: notebooks are read-only consumers of{% if include_core_etl %} `core/` and{% endif %} the data dirs —
no pipeline logic lives here. Promote anything reusable into a real module
with tests.
